<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring My Community</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Park!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify keywords and phrases in texts. Reading: Identify simple story elements (e.g., characters, setting). Phonemic Awareness: Identify a spoken word's initial and final phoneme if these words have been previously encountered and are attached to a picture.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify keywords and phrases related to community places in simple texts (e.g., "park" or "market"). Can identify simple story elements in community-related texts by locating characters and settings (e.g., recognizing "the girl" at "the library"). Can identify the initial and final phoneme of community-related words and their corresponding letters when supported by a picture (e.g., identifying the /ʈʃ/ sound at the beginning and /tʃ/ sound at the end of "church" with the letters "CH" and "CH" when shown a picture of a church).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the topic of community places using visuals and familiar words from Lesson 1.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> "Community Flashcard Frenzy."</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows large flashcards of community places (park, market, school, library, church, store).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students chorally repeat the names of the places. Teacher asks, "Where is the park?" Students point to the flashcard.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> "Finger Reading."</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models reading a short sentence (e.g., "It's the park.") on a large card, pointing to each word as it is read slowly and clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a church and the word "church". Teacher explicitly points to 'ch' at the beginning and end, saying /ʈʃ/ for the initial sound and /tʃ/ for the final sound.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up a picture of a library: "Is this a market?" (Expected answer: "No!" or "It's a library!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up a flashcard with the word "park": "Can you play here?" (Expected answer: "Yes!" or "Play!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring My Community," focusing on a short, simple narrative for reading comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> "My Community Story Map." A large, illustrated poster with 3-4 simple sentences about community places. Genre: Simple narrative text.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example text: "This is a big park. A girl plays in the park. The market is next to the park. The church is across the street. It is a tall church."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students read and comprehend simple texts related to their immediate environment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher reads the story map aloud once, pointing to words and pictures. Then asks, "What is the story about? A park or a school?" (Students point to the 'park' picture on the map).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Reading:</b> The teacher reads a sentence from the story map (e.g., "The park is big."). Then shows a picture of a small park. "True or False?" (Students say "False!" or shake heads). Repeat with "The girl plays in the park." (Show girl playing in park). Students say "True!" or nod.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Keyword Identification:</b> Teacher says, "Find the word 'park'." Students point to 'park' in the text. Repeat for 'market', 'girl', 'church'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading. Students practice identifying keywords, simple story elements, and phonemes in structured reading activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: WH-questions (Where is it?), Present simple (It's the market), Adjectives + nouns (big park).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: park, market, school, library, church, store, big, small, play.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: Phonemic awareness /p/ in "park", /m/ in "market", /ch/ (/ʈʃ/ and /tʃ/) in "church" (identifying initial and final sounds with letters).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Reading: "Read and Match."</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of community places and words/short phrases (e.g., "the park," "a market," "big school"). They read each word/phrase and draw a line to the correct picture. Teacher monitors and provides support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Reading: "Sentence Scramble & Phoneme Fun."</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students get sentence strips (e.g., "The," "is," "park," "big") and put them in order to read "The park is big." They then read the full sentence.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After reading, for words like "church," "park," "market," students circle the initial letter(s) that match the beginning sound (e.g., 'ch' for church) and the final letter(s) that match the ending sound from a choice of letters on their worksheet, supported by a picture.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check Reading: "Community Find."</b> In pairs, students receive a simple community map with labels (e.g., "Park," "Library," "School"). Student A reads a clue (e.g., "Find the small market." or "Where is the library?"). Student B points to the correct place on the map. They take turns reading and pointing. Student B can answer: "It's the library."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring My Community," focusing on reading for specific information.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Community Scavenger Hunt."</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups. Each group receives a set of clue cards. Each card has a simple sentence describing a community place or activity (e.g., "I am a big place. You can play here. It is the park." or "Look for a tall building. It is a church.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read each clue card aloud in their group. After reading, they must identify the place described. They then find the corresponding picture (e.g., a flashcard placed around the classroom or on a large community map) that matches the description.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages students to read the clues carefully and discuss which community place it is. (E.g., "Where is the big park?" "It's here!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This task focuses on reading comprehension, identifying keywords and simple descriptive phrases (e.g., "big place," "play here," "tall building") to complete the scavenger hunt.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check reading comprehension and keyword/element identification, followed by student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and individual "Read and Identify" worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Reading Check & Phoneme Circle."</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a worksheet with 2-3 simple sentences and corresponding pictures. For example:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The boy is at the market." (with pictures of a boy at a market and a girl at a library). Students read and circle the correct picture.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It's a small park." (with pictures of a small park and a big park). Students read and circle the correct picture.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below each circled picture, for words like "park," "market," "church," students are asked to circle the initial and final letters (e.g., 'P' / 'k' for park, 'M' / 't' for market, 'Ch' / 'ch' for church) from a small selection provided, demonstrating phonemic awareness.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify keywords and phrases related to community places.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify simple story elements (e.g., character "boy," setting "market").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify initial and final phonemes with letter correspondence for practiced words (e.g., 'ch' for /ʈʃ/ and /tʃ/ in 'church').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read words like 'park' and 'market'." (Circle: Yes / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the girl at the library in a picture after reading." (Draw: Happy face / Neutral face / Sad face)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student self-reflection and teacher's critical evaluation of the lesson for future planning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you read today?" (Students can say or draw).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to read in our story? What was difficult?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What reading strategies worked best for 2nd graders (e.g., picture support, finger tracking, phoneme identification)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to identify keywords, phrases, and simple story elements in the texts? Did they grasp the initial and final phonemes with letter correspondence?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with identifying words or story elements, or specific phonemes?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading about community places. Next, in Lesson 3, students will actively speak and interact with each other to describe and ask about these community places, using the vocabulary and structures they read today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 2 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>